#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# mul

#% text
# `mul(a, b, ...)` is the product `a*b*...`; the operator `*` writes it, and a number before a name may omit it (`2x`).

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Products are kept in a canonical order and equal factors are collected into powers: `x*x` is `x^2`.

#% item
# A list argument multiplies element by element.

#% item
# A name followed by brackets is a call, never a product: `x(x - a)` is `x` applied to `x - a` (its head is `x`),
# also with a space, `x (x - a)`. Write the product with `*`: `x*(x - a)`. A number before a bracket multiplies:
# `2(x + 1)` is `2*(x + 1)`. The notebook marks a call of a name that is also used as a variable with a warning
# and offers to insert the `*`; a value that comes back holding such a call gets a note under it, and prints the
# call in brackets as a power's base, `(x(x - a))^(1/2)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
mul(2, 3, 4)
#% output
#= 24

#% cell
x*y*x
#% output
#= y*x^2

#% cell
[1, 2, 3]*[4, 5, 6]
#% output
#= [4, 10, 18]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A number times a list scales it:

#% cell
3*[1, 2, 3]
#% output
#= [3, 6, 9]

#% text
# A bracket after a name is a call, so the product needs its `*`:

#% cell
[head(x(x - a)), head(x*(x - a))]
#% output
#= [x, mul]

#% cell
[integrate(x*(x - a), x), integrate(x(x - a), x)]
#% output
#= [(1/3)*x^3 - (1/2)*a*x^2, integrate(x(x - a), x)]

#% text
# Its head with `apply`:

#% cell
apply(mul, range(5))
#% output
#= 120

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `add`, `pow`, `product`, `dot`
